{"name":"Radioactive Lawn","hex":"#65fe08","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"radioactive","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Exhibiting radioactivity.","Dangerous and disgusting, particularly of people or ideas."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any radioactive substance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/radioactive"},{"term":"lawn","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Ground (generally in front of or around a house) covered with grass kept closely mown.","An open space between woods."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To furnish with a lawn."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lawn"}],"entries":[{"title":"Radioactive decay","blocks":[{"text":"Radioactive decay is the process by which an unstable atomic nucleus loses energy by radiation. A material containing unstable nuclei is considered radioactive. Three of the most common types of decay are alpha, beta, and gamma decay. The weak force is the mechanism that is responsible for beta decay, while the other two are governed by the electromagnetic and nuclear forces."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/NuclearReaction.svg/330px-NuclearReaction.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radioactive_decay","wikidata":"Q3332922"},{"title":"Lawn","blocks":[{"text":"A lawn is an area of soil-covered land planted with grasses and other durable plants such as clover which is maintained at a short height with a lawn mower and used for aesthetic and recreational purposes. It is also commonly referred to as part of a garden."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/The_Lawn_UVa_2007.jpg/330px-The_Lawn_UVa_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawn","wikidata":"Q207766"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg/500px-Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg/1280px-Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg/500px-Pierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pierre and Marie Curie in their Paris laboratory, before 1907","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APierre_and_Marie_Curie.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Crookes_tube_xray_experiment.jpg/500px-Crookes_tube_xray_experiment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Crookes_tube_xray_experiment.jpg/500px-Crookes_tube_xray_experiment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Taking an X-ray image with early Crookes tube apparatus in 1896. The Crookes tube is visible in the centre. The standing man is viewing his hand with a fluoroscope screen; this was a common way of setting up the tube. No precautions against radiation exposure are being taken; its hazards were not known at the time.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACrookes_tube_xray_experiment.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Radioactivity_and_radiation.png/500px-Radioactivity_and_radiation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Radioactivity_and_radiation.png/500px-Radioactivity_and_radiation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Graphic showing relationships between radioactivity and detected ionizing radiati